MINIMUM COST TO HIRE K WORKERS
🟢 Problem Statement

You are given:

quality[] → quality of each worker

wage[] → minimum wage expectation of each worker

integer k → number of workers to hire

You must hire exactly k workers.

Rules:

All selected workers must be paid proportionally to their quality.

Each worker must be paid at least their minimum expected wage.

Return the minimum total cost to hire exactly k workers.

🔎 Example
Input:
quality = [10,20,5]
wage = [70,50,30]
k = 2

Output:
105.0

🟢 Key Insight (Very Important ⚡)

Each worker has a ratio:

ratio = wage[i] / quality[i]


If we choose a group of workers,
we must pay them based on the highest ratio in the group.

Why?

Because if we pick worker with highest ratio R,
then:

Payment of worker j = quality[j] * R


This ensures everyone gets at least their expected wage.

🟢 Strategy

Compute ratio for every worker.

Sort workers based on ratio ascending.

Use a max heap to maintain k smallest quality workers.

Keep track of total quality.

At every step:

Add worker’s quality

If size > k → remove largest quality

If size == k → compute cost:

cost = totalQuality * currentRatio


Take minimum.

🟢 Why Heap?

We want:

Lowest total quality

Because totalCost = totalQuality × maxRatio

To minimize cost:

Keep k workers with smallest total quality.

🟢 Algorithm Steps

Create list of workers with:

(ratio, quality)


Sort by ratio ascending

Initialize:

maxHeap for quality

totalQuality = 0

minCost = infinity

For each worker:

add quality to heap

add to totalQuality

if heap size > k:

remove largest quality

subtract from totalQuality

if heap size == k:

cost = totalQuality × current ratio

update minCost

Return minCost

🟢 Java Code
import java.util.*;

class Solution {

    public double mincostToHireWorkers(int[] quality, int[] wage, int k) {

        int n = quality.length;

        double[][] workers = new double[n][2];

        // Step 1: store ratio and quality
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            workers[i][0] = (double) wage[i] / quality[i];  // ratio
            workers[i][1] = quality[i];
        }

        // Step 2: sort by ratio
        Arrays.sort(workers, (a, b) -> Double.compare(a[0], b[0]));

        PriorityQueue<Integer> maxHeap =
                new PriorityQueue<>(Collections.reverseOrder());

        double totalQuality = 0;
        double minCost = Double.MAX_VALUE;

        for (double[] worker : workers) {

            double ratio = worker[0];
            int q = (int) worker[1];

            maxHeap.add(q);
            totalQuality += q;

            if (maxHeap.size() > k) {
                totalQuality -= maxHeap.poll();
            }

            if (maxHeap.size() == k) {
                double cost = totalQuality * ratio;
                minCost = Math.min(minCost, cost);
            }
        }

        return minCost;
    }
}

🟢 Line-by-Line Explanation
🔹 Store ratio
workers[i][0] = (double) wage[i] / quality[i];


Because payment must follow proportional rule.

🔹 Sort by ratio

We process from smallest ratio upward.

Why?

If current worker has ratio R,
then R becomes the highest ratio in that group.

🔹 Max Heap
PriorityQueue<Integer> maxHeap =
    new PriorityQueue<>(Collections.reverseOrder());


We remove largest quality to keep total minimum.

🔹 Add Worker
maxHeap.add(q);
totalQuality += q;

🔹 Maintain only k workers
if (maxHeap.size() > k)
    totalQuality -= maxHeap.poll();


Remove largest quality.

🔹 Compute Cost
double cost = totalQuality * ratio;


This ensures all workers get valid wage.

🟢 Dry Run

Input:

quality = [10,20,5]
wage = [70,50,30]
k = 2

Step 1: Compute ratios
Worker	Ratio	Quality
0	7.0	10
1	2.5	20
2	6.0	5
Step 2: Sort by ratio
Ratio	Quality
2.5	20
6.0	5
7.0	10
Step 3: Iterate
Worker 1 (2.5, q=20)

Heap = [20]
Total = 20
Not enough size

Worker 2 (6.0, q=5)

Heap = [20,5]
Total = 25
Size = 2 → Compute:

25 × 6.0 = 150


MinCost = 150

Worker 3 (7.0, q=10)

Heap = [20,5,10]
Remove 20

Heap = [10,5]
Total = 15

Cost:

15 × 7 = 105


MinCost = 105 ✅

🟢 Final Answer = 105
